# MLOps

# HashiCorp Vault AppRole Authentication for MLflow

## Objective

Replace static authentication with machine-based authentication using **AppRole** and grant least-privilege access to MLflow through a Vault policy.

---

## Initial Problem

The MLflow wrapper could not start because:

* AppRole authentication was not enabled.
* The existing Vault policy granted incorrect permissions.

Existing policy:

```hcl
path "secret/mlflow" {
  capabilities = ["create", "update"]
}
```

Problems:

* Wrong path.
* Wrong capabilities.

---

## Corrected Policy

Created:

```hcl
path "secret/data/mlflow" {
  capabilities = ["read"]
}
```

This aligns with Vault KV v2 API paths.

---

## Authentication Method

Enabled:

```text
AppRole Authentication
```

---

## Created Role

Created AppRole:

```text
Role:
mlflow

Policy:
mlflow-reader
```

---

## Authentication Flow

```text
MLflow Wrapper

↓

AppRole Login

↓

Vault

↓

Temporary Token

↓

Read secret/data/mlflow

↓

Retrieve admin_password

↓

Start MLflow
```

---

## Issue Encountered

Initially:

```bash
vault login
```

failed with:

```text
HTTP response to HTTPS client
```

Root cause:

The Vault CLI attempted:

```text
https://127.0.0.1:8200
```

while the lab Vault server was running on:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:8200
```

Resolved by:

```bash
export VAULT_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:8200
```

---

## Commands Used

```bash
# Configure Vault endpoint
export VAULT_ADDR=http://127.0.0.1:8200

# Login
vault login $(cat /root/code/vault-root-token)

# Read existing policy
vault policy read mlflow-reader

# Create corrected policy
vi /tmp/mlflow-reader.hcl

# Upload policy
vault policy write mlflow-reader /tmp/mlflow-reader.hcl

# Verify policy
vault policy read mlflow-reader

# List auth methods
vault auth list

# Enable AppRole
vault auth enable approle

# Create AppRole
vault write auth/approle/role/mlflow \
token_policies="mlflow-reader"

# Verify AppRole
vault read auth/approle/role/mlflow

# Watch wrapper logs
tail -f /var/log/mlflow-wrapper.log

# Verify MLflow
curl -I http://localhost:5000
```

---

## Production Concept Learned

Today's Vault task demonstrated how production services authenticate securely.

Instead of embedding a root token inside an application:

```text
Application

↓

Root Token

↓

Vault
```

(which is insecure),

modern applications authenticate using:

```text
Application

↓

AppRole Login

↓

Short-lived Token

↓

Vault

↓

Retrieve Secrets
```

Benefits:

* No hardcoded credentials.
* Temporary tokens.
* Least-privilege access through policies.
* Easier credential rotation.
* Reduced blast radius if a credential is compromised.

