# MLOps

# Production Secret Management with Vault AppRole

## Objective

Understand how production services securely authenticate with Vault using AppRole instead of long-lived root tokens.

---

## Previous Development Pattern

```text
Application

↓

Root Token

↓

Vault

↓

Read Secret
```

Problems:

* Long-lived credential
* High privilege
* Difficult to rotate
* Security risk

---

## Production Pattern

```text
Application

↓

AppRole Login

↓

Vault

↓

Short-lived Token

↓

Read Secret

↓

Start Service
```

---

## Components

### AppRole

Machine identity used for authentication.

### Role ID

Public identifier (similar to a username).

### Secret ID

Private credential (similar to a password).

### Vault Token

Temporary token issued after successful authentication.

### Policy

Defines exactly what the token can access.

---

## Complete Authentication Flow

```text
MLflow Wrapper
        │
Role ID + Secret ID
        │
        ▼
Vault AppRole Login
        │
        ▼
Temporary Token
        │
        ▼
Policy Evaluation
        │
        ▼
Read secret/data/mlflow
        │
        ▼
Retrieve admin_password
        │
        ▼
MLflow Starts
```

---

## KV v2 API Path Concept

One important concept learned today:

Writing a secret:

```bash
vault kv put secret/mlflow ...
```

internally stores it under:

```text
secret/data/mlflow
```

Therefore, Vault policies must reference:

```hcl
path "secret/data/mlflow"
```

instead of:

```hcl
path "secret/mlflow"
```

This distinction is specific to the KV v2 secrets engine.

---

## Why AppRole Is Used

AppRole enables secure, non-human authentication for services.

Benefits include:

* No hardcoded credentials.
* Short-lived authentication tokens.
* Fine-grained permissions through policies.
* Easy credential rotation.
* Reduced attack surface.

This is the preferred authentication mechanism for backend services such as MLflow, APIs, CI/CD pipelines, and batch jobs.

